From: ghc-darwin8-ppc project
Subject: [PATCH] array: word-align STUArray Bool's setByteArray# zeroing

ghc-darwin8-ppc: `STUArray Bool` is bit-packed but its `unsafeRead`
and `unsafeWrite` access memory via `readWordArray#` / `writeWordArray#`
at full-word granularity, while `newArray`'s `setByteArray#` only
initialises `bOOL_SCALE n = ceil(n/8)` bytes.  For any size that does
not align to a full machine word the trailing partial-word bytes are
left at whatever `newByteArray#` returned -- i.e. uninitialised heap
memory.

On big-endian targets (PPC32, SPARC, ...) the bit corresponding to
element 0 of the first word lives in the *last* memory byte of that
word, so `setByteArray#` writing only the low-address bytes zeroes
the wrong end and **every** read of elements 0..(SIZEOF_HSWORD*8 - 1)
of a sub-word `STUArray Bool` returns garbage.  The same problem
exists on little-endian targets for any size that doesn't align to a
full word (e.g. n=33 on 32-bit leaves bytes 5..7 of the second word
uninitialised, so elements 33..63 read garbage), but has gone
unnoticed because nursery allocations on 64-bit-LE platforms tend to
return zeroed pages that mask the issue.

The fix is to round the byte count up to a whole machine word for both
the `newByteArray#` allocation and the `setByteArray#` zeroing call.
`newByteArray# n` already rounds its payload up to a word internally,
so passing the rounded byte count to both is safe.

A 3-line standalone reproducer (sz=8, init=False) reproduces on
pmacg5 (PPC32 unreg) at an ~84% rate under default RTS and 87% under
`-A1m -G1`; the fix drops both to 0/10001 iterations.  After
redeploying stage2 with the fix, the Big2.hs stage2-compile reproducer
that produced 152-byte empty .o files for the past ten sessions now
produces a fully-populated 46340-byte .o under both default RTS and
`-A1m -G1`.  See `docs/sessions/2026-05-15-session-52-stuarray-scope`
for the bisection and minimal repros that pinned this.

This is an upstream GHC bug, not a port-specific one -- the broken
code is identical in current GHC HEAD.  This patch is appropriate for
upstream submission once a smaller test case is prepared.

--- a/libraries/array/Data/Array/Base.hs
+++ b/libraries/array/Data/Array/Base.hs
@@ -1031,16 +1031,21 @@ instance MArray (STUArray s) Bool (ST s) where
     {-# INLINE getNumElements #-}
     getNumElements (STUArray _ _ n _) = return n
     {-# INLINE newArray #-}
+    -- See Note [STUArray Bool word-aligned initialization]
     newArray (l,u) initialValue = ST $ \s1# ->
-        case safeRangeSize (l,u)                   of { n@(I# n#) ->
-        case bOOL_SCALE n#                         of { nbytes# ->
-        case newByteArray# nbytes# s1#             of { (# s2#, marr# #) ->
-        case setByteArray# marr# 0# nbytes# e# s2# of { s3# ->
+        case safeRangeSize (l,u)                          of { n@(I# n#) ->
+        case bOOL_WORD_SCALE n#                           of { nbytes# ->
+        case newByteArray# nbytes# s1#                    of { (# s2#, marr# #) ->
+        case setByteArray# marr# 0# nbytes# e# s2#        of { s3# ->
         (# s3#, STUArray l u n marr# #) }}}}
       where
         !(I# e#) = if initialValue then 0xff else 0x0
     {-# INLINE unsafeNewArray_ #-}
-    unsafeNewArray_ (l,u) = unsafeNewArraySTUArray_ (l,u) bOOL_SCALE
+    -- Allocate a word-rounded number of bytes so the trailing partial
+    -- word's bits never read uninitialised memory through unsafeRead /
+    -- unsafeWrite, which work at word granularity.  See Note [STUArray
+    -- Bool word-aligned initialization].
+    unsafeNewArray_ (l,u) = unsafeNewArraySTUArray_ (l,u) bOOL_WORD_SCALE
     {-# INLINE newArray_ #-}
     newArray_ arrBounds = newArray arrBounds False
     {-# INLINE unsafeRead #-}
@@ -1355,6 +1360,44 @@ wORD_SCALE   n# = safe_scale scale# n# where !(I# scale#) = SIZEOF_HSWORD
 dOUBLE_SCALE n# = safe_scale scale# n# where !(I# scale#) = SIZEOF_HSDOUBLE
 fLOAT_SCALE  n# = safe_scale scale# n# where !(I# scale#) = SIZEOF_HSFLOAT

+-- Note [STUArray Bool word-aligned initialization]
+-- ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
+-- @STUArray Bool@ is bit-packed: the @MArray@ instance's @unsafeRead@
+-- and @unsafeWrite@ use @readWordArray#@ / @writeWordArray#@, which
+-- operate at full-word granularity (@SIZEOF_HSWORD@ bytes).  The byte
+-- count returned by @bOOL_SCALE@ is @ceil(n/8)@, which for @n < 32@ on
+-- a 32-bit target (or @n < 64@ on a 64-bit target) is strictly less
+-- than one machine word.  @newByteArray#@ does *not* zero its payload,
+-- so any bytes within the trailing partial word that @setByteArray#@
+-- did not write contain stale heap memory.  On big-endian targets the
+-- bit corresponding to element 0 of the first word lives in the *last*
+-- byte of that word in memory, so @setByteArray#@ writing only the
+-- low-address bytes zeros the wrong end and every read of elements
+-- 0..(SIZEOF_HSWORD * 8 - 1) returns garbage.  On little-endian
+-- targets the same problem exists for any size that does not align to
+-- a full word (e.g. n=33 on a 32-bit target leaves bytes 5..7 of the
+-- second word uninitialised, so elements 33..63 read garbage).  The
+-- bug had not been observed because nursery allocations on the more
+-- common 64-bit-LE platforms tended to return zeroed pages, masking
+-- the issue.  See session 52 of the ghc-darwin8-ppc project for the
+-- bisection and minimal repro that pinned this.
+--
+-- The fix is to round @bOOL_SCALE@'s byte count up to a whole word
+-- when allocating *and* when calling @setByteArray#@, so the trailing
+-- partial word is always fully initialized.  @newByteArray#@ already
+-- rounds its payload up to a word boundary internally, so the rounded
+-- byte count fits within the allocated region.
+bOOL_WORD_SCALE :: Int# -> Int#
+#if SIZEOF_HSWORD == 4
+-- (n + 31) >> 5 words * 4 bytes/word
+bOOL_WORD_SCALE n# = ((n# +# 31#) `uncheckedIShiftRA#` 5#) `uncheckedIShiftL#` 2#
+#elif SIZEOF_HSWORD == 8
+-- (n + 63) >> 6 words * 8 bytes/word
+bOOL_WORD_SCALE n# = ((n# +# 63#) `uncheckedIShiftRA#` 6#) `uncheckedIShiftL#` 3#
+#else
+# error "unsupported SIZEOF_HSWORD"
+#endif
+
 safe_scale :: Int# -> Int# -> Int#
 safe_scale scale# n#
   | not overflow = res#
